b/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493835 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493835 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186493449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493835 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "741e1c0e-e03a-5747-adb3-6e19f296eab1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493845 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493845 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493845 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493845 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493845 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493845 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493845 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493845 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186493644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493845 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8bc94f58-c888-5f48-8bc9-61a9a2954b43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493908 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493908 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79732" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493908 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493908 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSolve the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493908 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2dd7bee1-71b3-5685-9b9f-df2041d5d283" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493908 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=2 \\\\ {c}_{1}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n5\\cdot 6+{c}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-28.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution for the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =6\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+(-28)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}30 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}-28 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493957 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493957 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161046" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493957 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493957 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨\\text{__________},\\,\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(z,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\sqrt{z-1}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [1,10].$ What is the first component of $r?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493957 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7e0b385-be5e-5415-b9aa-b210f7036677" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493957 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/04/04de4ea7dc617be1429c596812ce4f450c2cdaafd8d94d86f75e68a6743acba9.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(z)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(z)=\\sqrt{z-1}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [1,10].$ \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [1,10],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493968 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493968 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140566" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493968 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493968 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493968 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dfb0578c-1343-53e2-a376-085feaa6ce26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493968 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493982 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186494054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493982 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1c778fa9-95bd-5205-85c3-55aecdf8bb5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493982 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186134551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493984 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186494005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493984 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "26b7b285-8b87-524a-acc3-8f6ad9b2bc51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493984 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186493957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494005 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186494039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494005 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5c8fa560-f511-5232-9190-5ad13dbecf87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494005 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186389442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494012 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186493984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494012 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "69dce80a-516d-5704-9e0c-f853f687f3c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494012 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186493968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494039 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "982b4c12-887f-5e66-8054-16e26a5f9fff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494039 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494046 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186494053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494046 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a5c706c5-3ca3-5218-b66a-dfcd2740c675" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494046 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186415681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494053 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186494012 #edb/ref 17592186045417